WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.640
Model-serving performance depends on several interacting factors: model size,

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.710
quantization, context length, available memory, CPU, GPU, disk speed, thermal behavior,

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:10.745
and concurrent use.

00:00:10.745 --> 00:00:14.975
A model that feels responsive for one user on a workstation may not be suitable for a

00:00:14.975 --> 00:00:15.730
shared server,

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:19.400
a heavier prompt workflow, or multiple simultaneous clients.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:24.320
Operators should watch memory, disk, CPU, GPU, and temperature while evaluating a model.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.168
Memory pressure can cause slow responses, failed loads, or system instability.

00:00:28.168 --> 00:00:30.343
Disk pressure can affect model pulls and logging.

00:00:30.343 --> 00:00:33.745
CPU and GPU pressure can change latency expectations.

00:00:33.745 --> 00:00:37.170
Thermal throttling can make performance worse over time even if the first few requests

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.415
look fine.

00:00:38.415 --> 00:00:42.600
Context length also matters. Longer prompts and larger conversation histories require more

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.629
work and more memory.

00:00:43.629 --> 00:00:47.670
Keep-alive behavior and loaded models can improve responsiveness because a model does not

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.070
need to load for every request,

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:50.860
but they can also hold scarce memory.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:54.510
The best setting depends on whether the environment is optimized for interactive use,

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.830
occasional requests, or shared workloads.

00:00:56.830 --> 00:01:00.990
Capacity planning should be based on observed workloads rather than wishful assumptions.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:05.280
Measure representative prompts, expected concurrency, response latency, resource use,

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:06.840
and failure behavior.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.870
Then document what the environment can support.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:12.685
Performance planning turns local AI from a pleasant demo into an operational service with

00:01:12.685 --> 00:01:13.890
realistic expectations.
